// @test: chained method/member access on a Vec returned by a by-value-struct-argument call (#412, #427)
// @description: a method call chained directly onto a call whose argument is a by-value
//   struct compiled to nothing at all -- `f(x)` was evaluated in LHS ("pointer") mode,
//   which pushed the receiver's pointer semantics down into the call's own argument
//   evaluation, so `x` reached `f` as its address ("Argument type mismatch for call to
//   f. Expected Big but got ptr") and the statement was dropped: `n` was never assigned
//   and the program printed nothing but compile diagnostics. A call-result receiver has
//   no storage slot, so it is now evaluated normally and the returned Vec is materialized
//   into its own temporary. #427 item 1 extends the same rule to MEMBER access on an
//   ownership-handle call result: the receiver `hs.get(0)` is an `our<A>` whose type lives
//   in the semantic table, not in the LLVM value map, so the member lookup now falls back
//   to the receiver expression's type -- `hs.get(0).v` resolves the payload struct instead
//   of failing to determine it. The bound form (`g<our<A>> = hs.get(0)`) is asserted
//   alongside the chained one.
// @category: ownership, vec, struct
// @expect-output: chained len=5 bound field=7 chained field=7
// @expect-return: 0

share(all)
struct Big {
    a<Vec<Int>>,
    b<Int>
}

share(all)
f(b<Big>)<Vec<Int>> -> {
    return b.a
}

share(all)
struct A { v<Int> }

main()<Int> -> {
    v<Vec<Int>> = Vec()
    for (i in 0..4) { v.push(i) }
    x<Big> = Big { a = v, b = 1 }
    n<Int> = f(x).len()

    hs<Vec<our<A>>> = Vec()
    hs.push(our(A { v: 7 }))
    g<our<A>> = hs.get(0)
    println("chained len=" + n.to_string() + " bound field=" + g.v.to_string() +
            " chained field=" + hs.get(0).v.to_string())
    return 0
}
